From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0409-motion-01-occlusion-reads.patch

Source: 0409-motion-01-occlusion-reads.patch
Moved by hand: without its FrontDmaReadFast hunk, as 0399: that fast path is the left-out 0353's

and gives up after 2 s

Core patch, after 0406. Part port, part ours. Camera-move glitches: motion-glitches.md,
track-motion-fix.md.

Already in T26, untouched here (read in the source, names found in T26's eboot):
EDEN_NX2_QUERY_DMA_TRACE and =1 (0317), =2, which is Lichii PR 215 (0399), EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE, both
halves of 5b89a3daf (0110). No saved PS5 run shows any of them in use.

Missing, added here as =3:

1. A limit on the wait. Port of A-Man-With-A-Plan-dev's 08b5ed82eb, his review on PR 215. Ours, like
   the PR, has none, and keeps its wait state on the stack: a lost operation hangs the GPU thread for
   good. SignalAndWaitFor waits in 5 ms slices, submits deferred work again, gives up after 2 s and
   keeps the state on the heap. Changed from the source: it sits beside SignalAndWait in the fence
   manager, and only =3 uses it.

2. Ours, from reading only, never seen in a log. =2 can hand a macro a 0. SemiFlushQueryDirty writes
   a final value before its operation adds the carried count (query_cache.h). A sync abandons the
   running query (PresyncWrites), so a report with no draw after it has final value 0 plus that
   count, and the early write sends the 0 with no wait. PR 215 has this path too. Pausing keeps
   carried counts alive across submissions, so it matters more with the pause on. =1 has no early
   write, but waits before the buffer cache flush, which can put older words back. =3 goes where =2
   goes, after the flush, and waits first.

Use it with EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE=1. A wait submits what is queued (fence or FlushCommands, then
SubmitExecution, EndPendingOperations), and without the pause each submission resets the counter,
the bug of #121. PR 215's base always pauses.

Cost: =3 waits on every report still registered, landed ones too, as =1 does. PR 215 counted 976
such reads in 2.5 minutes on NVIDIA. The PS5's rate is unknown; the trace gives it.

Evidence for the lead, reported, not ours: PR 215, flashes gone (author, NVIDIA, Linux, Low). ajcohn716
on PR 215, RX 7800 XT, RADV: 71 stale of 157 sampled, 0 of 120 with it; white vegetation not confirmed
fixed. Feralnet on PR 215 never saw the flashes without it, so that report proves no fix.

Risk: the pause alone hung an RX 9070 XT with the split on, at the save select (#121). The 256-slot
bound was meant to end that; no AMD retest with the split on is reported. If the wait gives up, the
read sees older words, as it does today, and the log says "Query DMA wait gave up".

Not ported: the log capy-ai asked for when the 256-slot bound fires (#121), upstream never added it.
166c21d96c and PR 243 are speed only.

Unset, 0, 1, 2: nothing changes. Memory: one small allocation per wait, at =3 only.
Clang syntax check, PS5 target, no warnings: vk_rasterizer, vk_fence_manager, maxwell_3d, macro, on
core16 plus 0182-0406. The saved header overlay was older than that tree, so vk_scheduler.h,
buffer_cache.h and texture_cache.h came from the tree. A deliberate typo in the new function fails
the check. The function's own text ran in a small harness under ThreadSanitizer and AddressSanitizer:
on time, lost, and late after giving up. Not built, not run on a PS5.

